# devDependencies are BUILD dependencies for this app, so install them always.
#
# `next build` runs PostCSS, which requires @tailwindcss/postcss, and the type
# check needs typescript and the @types packages. All four live in
# devDependencies, which is correct — they are not needed at runtime.
#
# The trap: the deployment sets NODE_ENV=production, hosts apply that during the
# build as well as at runtime, and `npm ci` then omits devDependencies. The
# first real deploy of this app died on:
#
#     Error: Cannot find module '@tailwindcss/postcss'
#
# Measured, not inferred — `npm ci --dry-run` reports the same package count
# either way and is useless as a probe. A real install of this lockfile into an
# empty directory:
#
#     NODE_ENV=production npm ci                 @tailwindcss/postcss MISSING
#                                                typescript           MISSING
#     NODE_ENV=production npm ci --include=dev   both PRESENT
#
# The build command also passes --include=dev, which is the visible, explicit
# version of this. This file is the belt to that braces: it makes the repository
# correct by default on any host and under any build command, so the next person
# to create a service does not rediscover the same failure. Neither is redundant
# — the flag documents the intent where a reader will look, and this catches the
# case where the flag is missing.
#
# web/src/lib/deps.test.ts asserts the pins this installs; it does not assert
# this file, because npm config is not something a unit test can meaningfully
# read back.
include=dev
